Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are prominent solid adsorbents that combine well-defined adsorptive sites, fine-tuned pore sizes, decorated interior functionalities to achieve strong binding affinity, high selectivity, large capacity, and low regeneration energy for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.1-3 However, with respect to practical post-combustion capture, concerns arise from the competitive adsorption of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ against $\mathrm{CO_{2}},^{4}$ and from limitations in chemical stability upon exposure to acidic components of flue gas (e.g., $S\mathrm{O}_{x}$ and $\mathsf{N O}_{x}$ ).5 Thus, MOFs with exceptional chemical stability tolerant for steam, bases and acids are highly desirable,6 whose design mainly relies on the judicious selection of charged linkers, highvalence metal centers, and high-connectivity secondary building units (SBUs).1,7
Azolate linkers, such as imidazolate (IM), 1,2,3-triazolate $\left(\mathrm{{Tz}}\right)$ , and 1,2,4-triazolate (TZ), have been used for constructing water- and basestable MOFs owing to their electrostatics, geometric rigidity, high basicity, and strong binding affinity (Scheme 1).8 Therefore, $\mathrm{{ZnF}(T Z)}$ and its isostructures9 (Figure 1a) constructed from rod - $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{Zn-F-Zn-F})_{n^{-}}$ SBUs and 3-connected TZ struts to form honeycomb-like channels are promising candidates for post-combustion $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture. Indeed, thermal stabilities of these isoreticular MOFs can be tuned by the functionality on the TZ struts, resulting in exceptional chemical stabilities that tolerate boiling water and aqueous acid/base $\mathrm{\boldmath~\pH}=1-12,$ ). Furthermore, their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ thermodynamic adsorption selectivity and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ kinetic adsorption selectivity can also be optimized by the chemical nature and amount of functional groups on the struts. The present findings shed light on the design of MOFs with exceptional chemical stability and tuned $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption kinetic selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from high-humidity flue gas.
Scheme 1. Strategies for constructing robust MOFs with azolate linkers: 1) Strong binding affinity to metal center; 2) steric shielding of metal center; 3) acid buffering functionality.
MOF samples were prepared via microwave-assisted solvothermal syntheses yielding materials with high crystallinity and uniform particle size (Section S1 in supporting information, SI). Crystallinity and phase purity of MOFs were validated by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD, Figure 1b), and the prism-shaped morphology and micro-sized crystals were confirmed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Figure S1). Thermal gravimetric analyses (TGA, Figure 1c) conform high thermal stabilities (up to $340{\-}440^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) with the trend of $\mathrm{ZnF(TZ)<ZnF(aTZ)}$ $<\mathrm{ZnF(daTZ)<ZnF(dmTZ)}$ ( $\mathbf{\lambda}_{\mathrm{aTZ}}=3$ -amino-1,2,4-triazolate, ${\bf d a T Z}={\bf\Phi}$ 3,5-diamino-1,2,4-triazolate, $\mathrm{dmTZ}~=~3,5$ -dimethyl-1,2,4-triazolate), which can be attributed to the electron-donating effect of the appended functional groups that strengthen TZ’s metal-binding affinity.8b Water stabilities of the frameworks were demonstrated by immersing $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ in boiling water, in which the structure maintained its crystallinity for at least 5 days (Figure 1d). Strikingly, this also tolerated aqueous acids and bases with $\mathrm{\pH}$ values ranging from 1 to 12, retaining both their crystallinity (Figure 1e) and porosity (Figure S2) for more than 20 hours. Similar chemical stabilities were tested for $\mathrm{{ZnF}(T Z)}$ and $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{aTZ})$ following the same protocol (Figures S3-S6 and Table S1). Such excellent chemical stability is essential for practical applications in post-combustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas, typically containing $75\%$ ${\bf N}_{2},$ $15\%$ CO2, $5\%H_{2}O$ , $3\mathrm{-}4\%\mathrm{O_{2},800\ p p m{S O_{2}},500\ p p m{N O_{{x}}}}.$
Permanent porosity of the frameworks was confirmed by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $195\mathrm{K}$ showing type I adsorption isotherms (Figure S7). Based on Monte-Carlo (MC) simulation by fitting their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $273\mathrm{K}$ (Figure S9), specific surface areas (890– $970\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{cm}^{3},$ ), pore volumes $\left(0.119-0.21\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ , and pore size distribution (PSD, $0.42\mathrm{-}0.60~\mathrm{nm}$ Figure S10, Figures S11–S13 and Table S2) were estimated, featuring ultramicroporosity suitable for physical adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ .
Coverage-dependent isosteric heats of adsorption (Figure S27) were calculated via the Clausius-Clapeyron equation by fitting their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at 273, 283, and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figures S18–S20, Figures S24–S26). Functionalized with amino groups, $Z\mathrm{nF}(\mathrm{aTZ})$ and $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ exhibit similar zero-coverage heat of adsorption ${\left(>30\right.}$ $\mathbf{kJ}/\mathbf{mol},$ ) significantly higher than that of the pristine $\mathrm{{ZnF}(T Z)}$ (24 $\mathrm{kJ/mol}\dot{}.$ ). This result supports the positive effect of amino functionalities for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.
The uptake capacity was estimated from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 2a), which was found to be related to the amount of amino groups in the channel. The pore size effect is ruled out by the lowest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake observed in $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{dmTZ})$ . Among the series, $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ represents the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $40\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{srp\cm}^{-3}$ $\mathrm{'1.8~mmol~cm^{-3}},$ , though it is not high compared with the benchmark MOFs (Table S3). We evaluated the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption selectivity (Figure 2b) based on the ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ( $V:V=15:85)$ mixture by fitting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 0–101 $\mathbf{kPa}$ (Figures S32–S34, Section S5 in SI). $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ exhibits $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity as high as 120, which is 8 times higher than that of the pristine $\mathrm{{ZnF}(T Z)}$ .
Water adsorption isotherms for all MOFs were collected at $298\mathrm{K}$ via the static volumetric method (Figure S8). Except for $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{dmTZ})$ , all MOFs display significant water uptakes of $250{-}450~\mathrm{cm^{3}s r p/c m^{3}}$ . The higher heat of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ adsorption (Figures S21–S23 and S28–S31) and the $_{\mathrm{H_{2}O/C O_{2}}}$ thermodynamic selectivity (Figure S38) indicate competitive adsorption of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ over $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . However, dynamic gas-adsorption breakthrough experiments in both dry and humid conditions $(99\%\mathrm{RH})$ have shown similar breakthrough time and kinetics (Figure $2\mathsf{c}$ ). Humidity-dependent $33\%$ , $53\%$ , and $99\%$ RH) $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ breakthrough curves for $\mathrm{{ZnF}(T Z)}$ , $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{aTZ})$ , and $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ were collected, respectively, (Figures S39-S44), which showed up to $20\%$ enhancement of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture under humid conditions (Figures S44-S45, and Table S5).
We next turned to study the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ( $(15\%)$ and $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (33, 53, and $95\%$ RH) adsorption kinetics for these MOFs using gravimetric dynamic vapor/gas adsorption using $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ as carrier gas (Figures S47–S52, Table S6). Significantly faster $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption kinetics are generally observed compared to those for $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ . Thus, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ kinetics selectivity can be estimated up to 70 (Figure 2d).10 We also studied the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake after pre-treatment with water vapor ( $10{-}30\%$ RH), and its $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption kinetic was found not to be affected (Figure S56). In contrast, pre-adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ will slow down water uptake (Figure S59, Table S7). This phenomenon can be attributed to the distinct adsorptive sites11 at the channel center for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure S68) and at the corner for $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (Figure S69) as indicated by GCMC simulations (Section $S10\mathrm{in}S\mathrm{I}\stackrel{\cdot}{}$ ). The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake enhancement in the presence of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is also supported by density functional theory (DFT) calculation, showing $25\text{\textperthousand}$ increment of heats of adsorption (Figures S70–S71).
Cycling breakthrough curves of $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ for closely mimic flue gas $\mathrm{\tilde{N}}_{2}80\%$ $\mathrm{6,15\%CO_{2},5\%O_{2},1000p m S O_{2},500p p m N O_{2})}$ exhibit a negligible decrease in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dynamic capacity even after 50 cycles (Figure 2e). Cycling breakthrough curves of $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ pellets represent cycling performance and faster adsorption kinetics for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ( $\nu:V=$ 15:85) mixed gas under $85\%$ RH at $298\mathrm{K}$ and flow rate of $10\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 2f). The regeneration process of $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ is actualized by just purging with Ar gas at $298\mathrm{K}$ for $0.5\mathrm{h}$ (Figures S62 and S65) to fully remove the adsorbates, while zeolite 13X showed drop of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture capacity (Figure S66) with the same protocol. Cycling dynamic water vapor adsorption for $\mathrm{ZnF}(\mathrm{daTZ})$ was performed with constant uptake capacity for 25 cycles regenerated by just purging dry $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ gas at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure S53). All results presented here suggest MOFs can function as designable solid adsorbents for high-humidity flue gas $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture requiring only minimal energy input.
